    doc /* The compilation pipeline operates within Complexity Class $`\mathbf{P}`$. Token scanning, symbol table interning, and schema set membership verification execute in deterministic linear time $`O(N)`$ with respect to total input schema token count $`N = |\text{Tokens}(\mathcal{S})|`$. Synthetic topological anchor generation and path hashing execute in $`O(V + E)`$ where $`V = |\text{Nodes}(\mathcal{A})|`$ and $`E = |\text{Edges}(\mathcal{A})|`$ represent AST hierarchy vertices and containment edges. Foundational schema ingestion and validation require no combinatorial search, bypassing NP-hard frontiers (e.g., NP-1 through NP-8) and guaranteeing total-function polynomial termination. ---
                      The surjective lexical provenance verification pipeline executes within Complexity Class $`\mathbf{P}`$. During schema parsing and lexical ingestion, source byte span capture and initial node registration operate in $`O(1)`$ amortized time per token, bounded by $`O(N)`$ for total input token count $`N = |\text{Tokens}(\mathcal{S})|`$. Synthetic topological anchor generation via RFC 4122 UUIDv5 path hashing operates in $`O(V + E)`$ where $`V = |\text{Nodes}(\mathcal{A})|`$ and $`E = |\text{Edges}(\mathcal{A})|`$. Positive provenance verification traversing $`\text{Nodes}(\mathcal{A})`$ executes in deterministic linear time $`O(V)`$ via direct handle and table lookups. Surjective range completeness verification across all parsed schema nodes executes in $`O(V + |\text{ASTNodes}(\mathcal{S})|)`$ using a dense index coverage table or bitset tracking array. Auxiliary memory consumption is strictly bounded by $`O(V + |\text{ASTNodes}(\mathcal{S})|)`$ for storing provenance mapping descriptors and source span coordinate tables. Unlike NP-complete scheduling or invariant satisfiability problems (e.g., NP-1 temporal consistency or NP-3 subtyping lattices), lexical provenance verification is strictly polynomial and free of combinatorial backtracking. ---
                      - **Complexity Class**: $`\mathbf{P}`$ (Deterministic Polynomial Time). - **Asymptotic Bounds**: - Pre-emission canonical sorting of $`M`$ declarations: $`O(M \log M)`$ time via total-order key comparison over interned symbol paths. - Streaming serialization, SHA-256 hashing, and in-memory diff verification across $`N`$ bytes: deterministic $`O(N)`$ linear time. - Aggregate emission pipeline complexity: bounded by $`O(M \log M + N)`$ time and $`O(N)`$ auxiliary memory within Complexity Class $`\mathbf{P}`$. - **NP Frontier Reference**: Decoupled from NP-hard scheduling frontiers (such as NP-7 LUMI IR register allocation); canonical sorting executes over acyclic AST structures with zero combinatorial backtracking. ---
                      Dynamic path resolution and validation execute in Complexity Class $`\mathbf{P}`$. Upward workspace root discovery executes in $`O(D)`$ time for directory nesting depth $``D \le D_{\max} = 64``$. Path normalization, component parsing, and prefix containment verification execute in deterministic linear time $`O(|P|)`$ with respect to path character length $`|P|`$, performing zero recursive filesystem traversal. Cryptographic digest verification executes in $`O(M)`$ time for total artifact byte size $`M`$. Auxiliary space is bounded by $`O(|P|)`$ for canonical path buffers. Path operations are strictly polynomial ($`O(|P|) \in \mathbf{P}`$) with no traversal of NP decision frontiers (NP-1 through NP-8). ---
                      The bootstrap environment validation and isolation mechanisms execute within Complexity Class $`\mathbf{P}`$. Startup environment inspection and whitelist filtering execute in $``O(|\mathcal{V}_{\text{env}}|)``$ deterministic time, where $``|\mathcal{V}_{\text{env}}|``$ is the number of ambient environment variables presented at process launch. Dynamic workspace path confinement checks execute in $`O(1)`$ amortized time per path evaluation (or $`O(|P|)`$ with respect to path string length $`|P|`$). Total auxiliary memory overhead for environment isolation verification is bounded by $`O(1)`$, requiring zero dynamic heap growth outside the compiler linear memory arena. The isolation contract operates independently of combinatorial NP frontiers (such as NP-1 through NP-8), completing deterministically in polynomial time without backtracking. ---
                      The incremental dependency tracking and invalidation pipeline executes within Complexity Class $`\mathbf{P}`$. Forward reachability closure computation for $`\text{Invalidated}(M)`$ over DAG $`G = (V, E)`$ executes in deterministic linear time $`O(|V| + |E|)`$ via breadth-first search or topological order traversal. Cache lookup and reuse for unimpacted units execute in $`O(1)`$ amortized time per unit. Incremental AST recomputation scales strictly as $``O(N_{\text{inv}})``$ time, where $``N_{\text{inv}} = \sum_{v \in \text{Invalidated}(M)} |\text{Tokens}(v)| \le N``$, with $`N`$ denoting total model tokens. Peak auxiliary memory for the dependency DAG and cache structures is bounded by $`O(|V| + |E|)`$. Unlike downstream NP-hard scheduling (NP-7 in REQ-0180) or allocation problems (NP-2 in REQ-0147), DAG invalidation closure belongs strictly to deterministic polynomial time $`\mathbf{P}`$. ---
                      Multi-file compilation unit resolution and hierarchical package scoping execute strictly within Complexity Class $`\mathbf{P}`$. Constructing the package forest over $`|\mathcal{U}|`$ compilation units executes in deterministic $`O(|V| + |E|)`$ time for package vertices $`V = |\mathcal{P}|`$ and containment or import edges $`E`$. Symbol table population scales linearly as $`O(N)`$ for $``N = \sum_{P \in \mathcal{P}} |\text{Declared}(P)|``$, with $`O(1)`$ amortized interned lookups. Lexical ancestor scope resolution executes in $`O(D)`$ time with depth $``D \le D_{\max} = 16``$, bounded by $`O(1)`$ constant-time lookups per frame. Import ambiguity checking evaluates in $`O(K)`$ time for $``K = |\text{Imports}(P_{\text{src}})| \le |V|``$. NP Frontier Context: Although general subtyping lattice satisfiability and multiple-inheritance feature redefinition across general posets is NP-complete (Algorithmic Bound NP-3), lexical package scoping and symbol lookup form a strict hierarchical forest without backtracking, guaranteeing deterministic polynomial-time evaluation in $`O(M \cdot D) = O(M)`$ time for $`M`$ symbol reference sites. Auxiliary memory overhead is bounded by $`O(N)`$ for interned symbol identifiers, package symbol tables, and scope frame references. ---
                      The SSOT multi-file AST resolution and projection pipeline operates strictly within Complexity Class $`\mathbf{P}`$. Unified AST synthesis $``\mathcal{A}_{\text{SSOT}}``$ across $`M`$ schema files with $`N`$ total tokens executes in deterministic linear time $`O(N)`$ with multigraph linking bounded by $`O(|V| + |E|)`$. Each downstream projection $``\psi_k``$ traverses relevant subgraphs in $``O(|V_k| + |E_k|)``$ time, bounded across all $`K`$ targets by $`O(K \cdot (|V| + |E|))`$. On-disk drift verification across total byte size $``B = \sum_{k=1}^K |D_k^{\text{disk}}|``$ executes in linear time $`O(B)`$ via streaming SHA-256 hashing. Auxiliary memory consumption is strictly bounded by $`O(N + |V| + |E|)`$ for contiguous AST storage, symbol interner pools, and projection buffers. NP Frontier Context: While invariant contract satisfiability over general logical theories is NP-complete or undecidable (Algorithmic Bound NP-8), downstream SSOT projection and anti-drift evaluation execute as deterministic acyclic multigraph traversals without backtracking or combinatorial search, guaranteeing polynomial-time execution. ---
                      Arena memory operations execute within Complexity Class $`\mathbf{P}`$. Individual node allocation executes in strict $`O(1)`$ worst-case constant time via integer addition, alignment masking, and pointer comparison against capacity $`C`$. Token byte slice extraction executes in $`O(1)`$ constant time with zero string cloning. Bulk arena chunk deallocation and pointer reset execute in $`O(1)`$ constant time, independent of total allocated AST node count $``N = |\text{Nodes}(\mathcal{A}_{\text{arena}})|``$. Total front-end memory consumption is strictly bounded by $`O(N)`$ linear space proportional to AST node count and alignment padding, eliminating per-object dynamic heap metadata overhead. Arena allocation requires no combinatorial search, operating outside NP-complete frontiers (NP-1 through NP-8) and ensuring deterministic constant-time execution. ---
                      The RFC 4122 UUIDv5 generation pipeline executes strictly within Complexity Class $`\mathbf{P}`$. For a canonical path string of byte length $``L = |\text{Bytes}(\mathcal{N}_{\text{topological}})|``$, SHA-1 hashing processes $`16 + L`$ bytes across $`\lceil (L + 25) / 64 \rceil`$ fixed 512-bit compression blocks, taking deterministic linear time $``O(L) = O(|\mathcal{N}_{\text{topological}}|)``$. Truncation, version/variant bitwise masking, and formatting execute in strictly $`O(1)`$ constant time. For an AST graph $`\mathcal{A} = (\mathcal{V}, \mathcal{E})`$ with $`V = |\mathcal{V}|`$ nodes and total qualified path length $``L_{\text{total}} = \sum_{v \in \mathcal{V}} |\mathcal{N}_{\text{topological}}(v)|``$, computing UUIDv5 identifiers across all nodes using path memoization executes in deterministic time $``O(V + L_{\text{total}}) = O(N)``$, where $`N`$ is total token count. Auxiliary memory is bounded by $`O(1)`$ stack space and $``O(L_{\max})``$ scratch buffer where $``L_{\max} = \max_{v \in \mathcal{V}} |\mathcal{N}_{\text{topological}}(v)|``$, with zero heap allocations. The pipeline involves zero combinatorial search, bypassing NP-hard frontiers (e.g., NP-1 through NP-8) with guaranteed polynomial termination. ---
                      The compilation pipeline operates strictly within Complexity Class $`\mathbf{P}`$. Token scanning, lexical parsing, and AST construction execute in deterministic linear time $`O(N)`$ with respect to total input schema tokens $`N = |\text{Tokens}(s)|`$. Scope resolution, metamodel validation, and topological sorting operate in $`O(V + E)`$ time, where $`V = |\text{Nodes}(\mathcal{A})|`$ and $`E = |\text{Edges}(\mathcal{A})|`$ denote AST graph vertices and dependency edges. Diagnostic error recording requires $`O(1)`$ amortized time per entry, with deterministic sorting bounded by $`O(|\mathcal{D}| \log |\mathcal{D}|)`$ time where $`|\mathcal{D}| \ll N`$. Peak memory footprint is strictly bounded by $`O(N + V)`$ space in contiguous memory arenas. NP Frontier Reference: Decoupled from NP-hard combinatorial frontiers (such as NP-1 temporal consistency or NP-7 register allocation); baseline compilation pipeline passes execute strictly linear and linear-logarithmic traversals over acyclic AST structures without unbounded iteration or backtracking. ---
                      Work-stealing task execution operates within Complexity Class $`\mathbf{P}`$. For any computation task DAG with sequential work $``T_1``$ and critical-path span $``T_\infty``$, the bounded work-stealing scheduler achieves expected execution time: T_P \le \frac{T_1}{P} + O(T_\infty) where $``P = |P_{\text{workers}}| = \min(C_{\text{logical}}, N_{\text{max\_workers}})``$. Local deque operations execute in deterministic $`O(1)`$ time. Remote work-stealing attempts execute in $`O(1)`$ lock-free atomic compare-and-swap time per attempt, with total steal attempts bounded by $``O(P \cdot T_\infty)``$ with high probability. Space complexity is strictly bounded by $``O(P \cdot S_1)``$, where $``S_1``$ is peak sequential memory consumption. Parallel task execution over the AST graph bypasses combinatorial explosion; phases subject to NP-hard complexity (such as NP-4 STPA minimal hazard cut-sets per REQ-0135 or NP-5 state reachability per REQ-0124) delegate combinatorial subproblems to bounded solvers with explicit timeout cutoffs ($``\kappa_{\text{safety}}``$), guaranteeing polynomial overhead in work distribution. ---
                      The synchronization token error recovery engine operates within Complexity Class $`\mathbf{P}`$. Monotonic forward token skipping ($``\text{StreamPosition}_{t+1} \gt \text{StreamPosition}_t``$) inspects each token $`O(1)`$ times without backtracking, preserving deterministic $`O(N)`$ linear worst-case time complexity across token stream length $`N = |T|`$. Stack unwinding across $`K`$ recovery operations requires at most $``O(K \cdot H_{\text{max}})``$ operations for bounded stack depth $``H_{\text{max}} \le 256``$, bounding total unwinding time by $`O(N)`$. Cascading error suppression checks execute in $`O(1)`$ constant time via scalar subtraction. Total parser execution time is bounded by $`O(N)`$ with $``O(H_{\text{max}} + C_{\text{max\_errors}}) = O(1)``$ auxiliary space. Purely syntactic linear stream advancement operates strictly in $`\mathbf{P}`$, bypassing NP frontiers (NP-1--NP-8). ---
                      The format detection engine operates within Complexity Class $`\mathbf{P}`$. Classification of an individual file executes in strict $`O(K) = O(1)`$ constant time relative to total file size $`|F|`$, inspecting at most $`K = 512`$ bytes without heap allocation. File extension matching operates in $``O(L_{\text{ext}}) = O(1)``$ time for $``L_{\text{ext}} \le 16``$. Content prefix scanning executes via single-pass exact byte comparison over $`[0, \min(|F|, K)]`$, guaranteeing each byte is inspected at most $`O(1)`$ times with zero backtracking. For $`M`$ schema files, sequential detection time is $`O(M \cdot K) = O(M)`$ linear time;, parallel wall-clock time scales as $``O(\lceil M / N_{\text{max\_workers}} \rceil \cdot K) = O(M / N_{\text{max\_workers}})``$. Auxiliary memory is bounded by $`O(K) = O(1)`$ space per worker thread. Purely linear deterministic prefix scanning operates strictly in $`\mathbf{P}`$, bypassing all NP frontiers (NP-1--NP-8). ---
                      The table lexer executes within Complexity Class $`\mathbf{P}`$. Because transitions evaluate scalar byte matches without backtracking ($`\text{BacktrackCount} = 0`$) and regular expressions are strictly prohibited ($`\text{RegexEvaluations} = 0`$), each input byte is processed in $`O(1)`$ operations, guaranteeing strictly linear time complexity $`O(N)`$ where $`N`$ is input byte length. Stack operations execute in $`O(1)`$ time, and the pushdown stack depth is bounded by constant $`D \le 4`$, establishing an auxiliary memory bound of $`O(D) = O(1)`$. The streaming sink architecture writes directly to output buffers with $`O(1)`$ active memory overhead independent of file length $`N`$. The automaton requires zero backtracking search, strictly bypassing combinatorial NP-hard problem frontiers (e.g., NP-1 through NP-8) and guaranteeing total-function polynomial termination. ---
                      The table delimiter recovery engine operates within Complexity Class $`\mathbf{P}`$. Forward scanning is strictly monotonic ($``\text{ByteOffset}_{t+1} \gt \text{ByteOffset}_t``$), inspecting each byte $``b_k \in B``$ at most $`O(1)`$ times without backtracking for deterministic $`O(N)`$ worst-case time across stream length $`N = |B|`$. Row allocations and sentinel substitutions execute in $``O(K_{\text{max}})``$ time per row ($``K_{\text{max}} \le 64``$), bounded overall by $``O(M \cdot K_{\text{max}}) = O(N)``$ for $`M`$ rows. Cascading suppression checks execute in $`O(1)`$ scalar time. Auxiliary heap space is bounded by $``O(K_{\text{max}} + C_{\text{max\_errors}}) = O(1)``$ relative to $`N`$. Delimiter scanning operates strictly in $`\mathbf{P}`$, avoiding all NP frontiers (NP-1--NP-8). ---
                      Header key normalization and column binding operate strictly within Complexity Class $`\mathbf{P}`$. For each raw header $``H_j``$, string trimming, case folding, and punctuation stripping execute in a single pass in deterministic $``O(|H_j|)``$ time. For bounded header length $``|H_j| \le L_{\text{max}} = 256``$ bytes, normalization runs in $`O(1)`$ constant time. Canonical alias lookup against $``\mathcal{K}_{\text{canonical}}``$ runs in deterministic $`O(1)`$ time via a static perfect hash table or Trie. For a table with $``C \le C_{\text{max\_cols}} = 32``$ columns, computing binding vector $`\vec{\beta}`$ takes $``O(C \cdot L_{\text{max}}) = O(C) = O(1)``$ time. Injective collision checks and mandatory coverage evaluate over 64-bit integer masks in $`O(C)`$ time and $`O(1)`$ space. For $`M`$ data rows with total table byte size $`N`$, positional cell extraction via $`\vec{\beta}`$ executes in $`O(M \cdot C) = O(N)`$ time. Total table ingestion runs in deterministic $`O(N)`$ linear time in input size with $`O(C) = O(1)`$ auxiliary stack space ($`\le 128`$ bytes) and zero heap allocations during row ingestion. Bounded linear parsing operates strictly within $`\mathbf{P}`$ and bypasses all NP-complete combinatorial frontiers (NP-1--NP-8). ---
                      The table cell block token normalization pipeline operates strictly within Complexity Class $`\mathbf{P}`$. Deterministic prefix pattern matching against finite set $``\mathcal{P}_{\text{break}}``$ evaluates at most $``L_{\text{max}} = 6``$ bytes per position in $`O(1)`$ time. Monotonic forward stream traversal guarantees each byte in $``C_{\text{raw}}``$ is visited once, establishing a strict $`O(N)`$ time bound for $``N = |C_{\text{raw}}|``$. Auxiliary space complexity is $`O(1)`$ for streaming and $`O(K)`$ bounded arena storage for $`K \le N`$ token descriptors referencing existing byte buffer spans without heap reallocation. The algorithm requires zero combinatorial backtracking, operating strictly in $`\mathbf{P}`$ and remaining bounded well below NP-complete frontiers (e.g., REQ-0117 temporal interval consistency NP-1 or REQ-0135 minimal cut-sets NP-4). ---
                      Identifier sanitization and row-to-entity mapping operate within Complexity Class $`\mathbf{P}`$. For any raw identifier $``S_{\text{raw}}``$ with $``|S_{\text{raw}}| \le L_{\text{max}} = 256``$, whitespace trimming, character mapping $`\rho^*`$, underscore collapsing, and prefixing $``\pi_{\text{id}}``$ execute in a single forward pass in $``O(|S_{\text{raw}}|) = O(1)``$ time. Keyword lookup against $``\mathcal{K}_{\text{reserved}}``$ operates in $`O(1)`$ time via a static hash table. Sibling collision resolution executes in $`O(1)`$ amortized time via hash table lookups. For a table of $`M`$ rows and $`C \le 32`$ columns with total size $``N = \sum_{i=1}^M |\mathbf{r}_i|``$ bytes, row-to-entity synthesis executes in deterministic linear time $`O(N)`$ with $`O(M)`$ auxiliary arena storage. The transformation is strictly polynomial, bypassing NP-hard combinatorial search spaces (NP-1--NP-8). ---
                      Disambiguation of array indexing syntax executes within Complexity Class $`\mathbf{P}`$. Monotonic single-pass character scanning evaluates each byte of token $`S`$ in $`O(1)`$ operations, achieving deterministic $`O(|S|)`$ linear time without backtracking. For cell tokens bounded by $``|S| \le L_{\text{max\_cell}} = 256``$ bytes, evaluation executes in $`O(1)`$ constant time. Across a table of $`N`$ total bytes, ingestion executes in deterministic $`O(N)`$ linear time and $`O(1)`$ auxiliary stack space for rank $`K \le 8`$. Lexical character scanning bypasses NP-complete combinatorial frontiers (NP-1--NP-8), executing strictly in polynomial time. ---
                      Tabular schema ingestion for structural parts executes within Complexity Class $`\mathbf{P}`$. For a table of $`R`$ rows and $`C \le 32`$ columns, cell extraction and token parsing execute in $`O(R \cdot C) = O(R)`$ deterministic time. Multiplicity interval evaluation operates on strings of length $``L_M \le 32``$ in $`O(1)`$ time per row ($`O(R)`$ total). Contiguous AST node construction and symbol interning take $`O(1)`$ amortized time per row. Structural containment validation over graph $``G_{\text{contain}} = (V, E)``$ with $`|V| = R`$ and $`|E| \le R`$ executes in deterministic $`O(V + E) = O(R)`$ time via single-pass topological traversal. Space complexity is $`O(R)`$ auxiliary space. Combinatorial NP frontiers (NP-1--NP-8) are bypassed, as validation is restricted to acyclic forest topologies solvable in deterministic linear time. ---
                      Tabular schema ingestion for directed ports executes within Complexity Class $`\mathbf{P}`$. Monotonic single-pass scanning advances through $`N`$ total bytes in deterministic $`O(N)`$ time without backtracking. Header mapping, direction parsing $`\text{ParseDir}`$, and involution $``\tau_{\text{conj}}``$ execute in $`O(1)`$ constant time with zero dynamic memory allocation. Scoped port uniqueness checks perform hash lookups in amortized $`O(1)`$ time per row. Total ingestion across $`R`$ rows and $`C \le 32`$ columns executes in $`O(R \cdot C) = O(N)`$ time and requires $``O(C + C_{\text{max\_errors}}) = O(1)``$ auxiliary memory relative to $`N`$. The deterministic pass operates strictly in polynomial time and avoids NP-hard combinatorial frontiers (NP-1--NP-8). ---
                      Tabular attribute schema ingestion executes within Complexity Class $`\mathbf{P}`$. For $`R`$ rows and $`C \le 32`$ columns, each row requires $`O(1)`$ operations: column binding $`O(C) = O(1)`$, symbol interning $`O(1)`$, scalar type lookup $`O(1)`$, numeric boundary parsing $`O(1)`$, interval check ($`L \le U`$) $`O(1)`$, default containment check $`O(1)`$, and unit mapping into $`\mathbb{Q}^7`$ $`O(1)`$. Total sequential ingestion time is strictly linear: T(R) = O(R) Parallel execution across $`M`$ files using a bounded work-stealing pool with $``N_{\text{max\_workers}}``$ threads scales as $``O(R / N_{\text{max\_workers}})``$. Auxiliary memory is $`O(1)`$ heap overhead per row via arena allocation, with preallocated diagnostic sink bounded by $``O(C_{\text{max\_errors}}) = O(1)``$. Operates strictly in $`\mathbf{P}`$, orthogonal to NP frontiers (NP-1--NP-8). ---
                      Tabular schema ingestion for formal constraints executes within Complexity Class $`\mathbf{P}`$. For $`N`$ input bytes, $`R`$ rows, and $`C`$ columns ($``C \le C_{\text{max\_cols}} = 32``$), ingestion runs in deterministic linear time $`O(R) = O(N)`$. Per-row tasks--column lookup, symbol interning ($`O(1)`$ amortized), zero-copy slice extraction ($`O(1)`$), enum matching ($`O(1)`$), and envelope validation ($`O(1)`$)--require $`O(1)`$ auxiliary stack space. Downstream proof obligations under NP-8 (REQ-0197) and temporal networks under NP-1 (REQ-0117) are decoupled from ingestion, requiring zero backtracking. Dispatching $`K`$ tables across $``N_{\text{max\_workers}}``$ threads scales as $``O(\lceil K / N_{\text{max\_workers}} \rceil \cdot R_{\text{max}})``$. ---
                      The connection table ingestion engine operates within Complexity Class $`\mathbf{P}`$. Monotonic single-pass scanning of $`N`$ input bytes executes in $`O(N)`$ operations. Decomposing bounded endpoint paths ($``L_P \le 256``$) executes in $``O(L_P) = O(1)``$ deterministic time. Scalar symbol interning, self-loop evaluation, latency bound verification, and AST memory entity allocation execute in $`O(1)`$ amortized time per row. Total execution time across $`R`$ connection rows and $`C \le 32`$ columns is strictly linear in $`O(R \cdot C) = O(R) = O(N)`$. Auxiliary space complexity is $`O(R)`$ for AST storage, $`O(1)`$ stack frames, and $``O(C_{\text{max\_errors}}) = O(1)``$ for diagnostic records. Tabular connection ingestion executes in polynomial time $`\mathbf{P}`$ and avoids all combinatorial NP-complete frontiers (NP-1--NP-8). ---
                      The ingestion engine operates within Complexity Class $`\mathbf{P}`$. For byte length $`N`$ with $`R`$ rows and $`C \le 32`$ columns, lexical scanning and delimiter detection advance monotonically in $`O(N)`$ operations. Header binding $`\vec{\beta}`$ executes in $`O(C) = O(1)`$ time. Per row $``R_i``$, parameter parsing over $`P \le 32`$ tokens, symbol interning, state scope hash lookup, and arena node allocation execute in $`O(1)`$ amortized time. Total sequential runtime is strictly linear: T(R, N) \in O(N) = O(R \cdot C) = O(R) Parallel execution across $`K`$ tables on $``N_{\text{max\_workers}}``$ threads scales in $``O(\lceil K / N_{\text{max\_workers}} \rceil \cdot R_{\text{max}})``$. Auxiliary heap space is bounded by $`O(R)`$ arena storage and $``O(C_{\text{max\_errors}}) = O(1)``$ diagnostics. Downstream state reachability and temporal consistency verification are decoupled and addressed in Subsystems 6 and 7 (NP-1, NP-5). ---
                      The Proto3 AST ingestion engine executes in Complexity Class $`\mathbf{P}`$. Monotonic single-pass lexing and recursive descent parsing run in deterministic $`O(N)`$ linear time over $`N`$ input bytes using LL(1) lookahead. Identifier interning operates in $`O(1)`$ amortized time. Field tag validation per message with $`F`$ fields uses a dense bitset in $`O(F)`$ time, bounding aggregate validation across all messages by $`O(\sum F) \le O(N)`$. AST memory node allocation executes in $`O(1)`$ constant time. Message nesting recursion depth is bounded by $`D \le 64`$, requiring $`O(1)`$ auxiliary stack space. Parallel compilation across $`K`$ schemas on a bounded work-stealing pool ($``N_{\text{max\_workers}}``$) runs in $``O(\lceil K / N_{\text{max\_workers}} \rceil \cdot N_{\text{max}})``$ time. Deterministic linear parsing operates strictly in polynomial time, bypassing combinatorial NP-complete frontiers (NP-1--NP-8). ---
                      The OMG IDL AST ingestion engine executes in Complexity Class $`\mathbf{P}`$. Deterministic recursive descent parsing operates in $`O(N)`$ linear time over $`N`$ input bytes using LL(1) lookahead (bounded LL(2) for scoped symbols `A::B`). String interning runs in $`O(1)`$ amortized time. AST memory node allocation executes in $`O(1)`$ constant time. Parameter partitioning for $`M`$ parameters per operation executes in $`O(M)`$ time, bounding aggregate partitioning across the compilation unit by $`O(N)`$. Module hierarchy validation runs in $`O(D)`$ time with nesting depth $`D \le 64`$, bounding auxiliary stack space by $`O(1)`$. Parallel compilation across $`K`$ schemas on a bounded work-stealing pool ($``N_{\text{max\_workers}}``$) runs in $``O(\lceil K / N_{\text{max\_workers}} \rceil \cdot N_{\text{max}})``$ time. Deterministic linear parsing operates strictly in polynomial time, bypassing combinatorial NP-complete frontiers (NP-1--NP-8). ---
                      The OpenAPI schema ingestion engine operates within Complexity Class $`\mathbf{P}`$. Monotonic single-pass parsing of an input document of $`N`$ bytes executes in $`O(N)`$ operations without backtracking. Symbol interning for scalar tokens ($`L \le 256`$) executes in $`O(1)`$ amortized time. Lowering $`S`$ component schemas with $`F`$ properties evaluates in $`O(S + F) \le O(N)`$ time. Resolving local JSON Pointer references over schema graph $``G_{\text{ref}} = (V_{\text{schema}}, E_{\text{ref}})``$ with cycle memoization executes in $``O(V_{\text{schema}} + E_{\text{ref}}) = O(N)``$ time. Lowering $`P`$ path items with $`M`$ operations and $`K`$ parameters evaluates in $`O(P + M + K) \le O(N)`$ time. AST memory entity allocation executes in $`O(1)`$ time per node. Multi-file parallel compilation across $`K`$ documents on a bounded work-stealing pool with $``N_{\text{max\_workers}}``$ workers scales in $``O(\lceil K / N_{\text{max\_workers}} \rceil \cdot N_{\text{max}})``$. Auxiliary space complexity is $`O(N)`$ for AST storage and symbol tables, $``O(D_{\text{max}}) = O(1)``$ stack frames ($``D_{\text{max}} \le 64``$), and $``O(C_{\text{max\_errors}}) = O(1)``$ for diagnostic records. Ingestion executes in polynomial time $`\mathbf{P}`$ and avoids all combinatorial NP-complete frontiers (NP-1--NP-8). ---
                      The XSD and ADL XML ingestion engine executes in Complexity Class $`\mathbf{P}`$. Deterministic pushdown parsing scans $`N`$ bytes in $`O(N)`$ linear time without backtracking. Element hierarchy tracking maintains stack depth $`D \le 64 \ll N`$, requiring $`O(D) = O(1)`$ space. Lowering $`V`$ components/types, $`P`$ ports, and $`K`$ connectors requires $`O(V + P + K) \le O(N)`$ entity operations. Symbol interning and AST memory node allocation execute in $`O(1)`$ amortized and constant time respectively. Resolving endpoints across $`K`$ connections takes $`O(K)`$ time via scoped symbol tables. Parallel compilation across $`M`$ schemas on a bounded work-stealing pool ($``N_{\text{max\_workers}}``$) runs in $``O(\lceil M / N_{\text{max\_workers}} \rceil \cdot N_{\text{max}})``$ time. The engine executes strictly in polynomial time, bypassing combinatorial NP-complete frontiers (NP-1--NP-8). ---
                      The canonical SysML v2 textual emitter executes within Complexity Class $`\mathbf{P}`$. Depth-first AST traversal visits each node $``u \in \mathcal{V}_{\text{IR}}``$ once in deterministic $``O(|\mathcal{V}_{\text{IR}}|)``$ time. Sorting child entities by fully qualified name within scopes of size $``k_u``$ requires $``O(\sum k_u \log k_u) \le O(|\mathcal{V}_{\text{IR}}| \log k_{\text{max}})``$, reducing to $``O(|\mathcal{V}_{\text{IR}}|)``$ with pre-sorted scope tables (REQ-0032, REQ-0040). Token formatting and contiguous buffer appends scale linearly as $``O(L_{\text{bytes}}) = O(|\mathcal{V}_{\text{IR}}|)``$. Atomic persistence requires $``O(L_{\text{bytes}})``$ sequential stream writes and an $`O(1)`$ rename syscall. Total runtime is strictly linear in model size: $``T(|\mathcal{V}_{\text{IR}}|, L_{\text{bytes}}) \in O(|\mathcal{V}_{\text{IR}}| + L_{\text{bytes}}) = O(N)``$. Auxiliary space is $``O(L_{\text{bytes}}) = O(N)``$ for pre-allocated buffers and $``O(D_{\text{max}}) = O(1)``$ stack frames for nesting depth $``D_{\text{max}} \le 64``$. Parallel emission across $`K`$ units on a bounded work-stealing pool ($``N_{\text{max\_workers}}``$) runs in $``O(\lceil K / N_{\text{max\_workers}} \rceil \cdot N_{\text{max}})``$ time. Deterministic linear emission executes strictly in polynomial time $`\mathbf{P}`$, bypassing NP-complete frontiers (NP-1--NP-8). ---
                      The deterministic qualified-name symbol sorting and emission engine operates in Complexity Class $`\mathbf{P}`$. Let $`M = |\mathcal{D}|`$ denote total declarations across $`K`$ parent scopes, with scope $`j`$ containing $``m_j``$ declarations ($``\sum_{j=1}^K m_j = M``$). Let $`L`$ denote maximum qualified name length ($`L \le 1024`$ bytes). Comparing two qualified name strings requires $`O(L)`$ byte operations. Sorting declarations within scope $`j`$ via comparison sort requires $``O(L \cdot m_j \log m_j)``$ operations. Summed across all scopes: \sum_{j=1}^{K} O(L \cdot m_j \log m_j) \le O(L \cdot M \log M) Because $`L`$ is bounded by a fixed constant, sequential sorting runtime is strictly $`O(M \log M)`$., parallel sorting across independent package subtrees executes in $``O((M \log M) / N_{\text{max\_workers}} + M_{\text{root}} \log M_{\text{root}})``$ wall-clock time. Space complexity is $`O(M)`$ for declaration handle vectors in model memory with zero redundant string cloning. NP frontier reference: As a deterministic sorting transformation, the algorithm operates strictly in polynomial time $`\mathbf{P}`$, bypassing combinatorial NP-hard scheduling frontiers (NP-7, REQ-0180). ---
                      The multi-file cryptographic digest engine operates strictly within Complexity Class $`\mathbf{P}`$. For $``K = |\mathcal{F}_{\text{manifest}}|``$ tracked files with aggregate byte size $``B = \sum_{i=1}^K |F_{i}|``$, SHA-256 digest and line computations execute in $`O(B)`$ linear time via a single streaming pass. Lexicographical sorting of $`K`$ path descriptors of bounded length $``L_{\max} \le 1024``$ executes in $``O(K \log K \cdot L_{\max}) = O(K \log K)``$ operations. Manifest serialization and composite hashing execute in $`O(K)`$ time. Total sequential runtime is strictly $`O(B + K \log K) = O(B)`$ deterministic linear time in input byte size $`B`$ (since $`K \log K \ll B`$)., parallel wall-clock runtime scales as $``O(B / N_{\text{max\_workers}} + \max_{i} |F_{i}| + K \log K)``$. Auxiliary space is bounded by $`O(K)`$ for manifest records in AST storage and $`O(1)`$ streaming state per thread. The pipeline performs zero combinatorial search, bypassing NP-complete frontiers (NP-1 through NP-8) and ensuring polynomial termination. ---
                      The contiguous memory lowering engine executes within Complexity Class $`\mathbf{P}`$. Slot allocation executes in amortized $`O(1)`$ time via pointer bump and capacity check. Complete lowering of an AST with $``N = |\mathcal{V}_{\text{AST}}|``$ nodes executes in deterministic linear time $`O(N)`$ in a single traversal pass. Random slot access executes in worst-case $`O(1)`$ constant time via base-plus-offset address arithmetic $``\text{Base} + i \cdot S_{\text{slot}}``$. Sequential traversal executes in $`O(N)`$ time with hardware cache prefetching. Total memory footprint is strictly bounded by $`O(N)`$ linear space proportional to $``N \cdot S_{\text{slot}}``$ with zero per-node heap allocator overhead. Parallel lowering across $`K`$ units on bounded work-stealing threads ($``N_{\text{max\_workers}}``$) achieves $``O(\lceil K / N_{\text{max\_workers}} \rceil \cdot N_{\text{max\_unit}})``$ wall-clock scaling. Metamodel arena lowering operates entirely in polynomial time $`\mathbf{P}`$, serving as the zero-indirection substrate for downstream tractable graph passes (e.g., NP-3 type linearization in $`O(V+E)`$). ---
                      Node handle operations execute within Complexity Class $`\mathbf{P}`$: - **Handle Packing & Dereferencing**: Strict $`O(1)`$ worst-case constant time, executing one bounds check, one array indexing, and two scalar equality comparisons ($`\text{gen}`$ and $`\text{tag}`$). - **Generational Invalidation**: Strict $`O(1)`$ constant time via scalar increment upon slot reclamation. - **Graph Traversal**: Deterministic $`O(V + E)`$ linear time across $`V = |\text{Nodes}|`$ and $`E = |\text{Edges}|`$, with zero pointer chasing and contiguous memory cache locality. - **NP-Frontier Avoidance**: Addressing and dereferencing operate without backtracking or combinatorial search, completely avoiding NP-complete complexity frontiers (e.g., NP-3 subtyping lattices or NP-7 register allocation). ---
                      String interning operates in Complexity Class $`\mathbf{P}`$. Initial interning of string $`s`$ takes deterministic $`O(|s|)`$ time to hash and copy bytes into contiguous buffer $``\mathcal{B}_{\text{pool}}``$. Subsequent lookups require $`O(|s|)`$ time for hash computation and expected $`O(1)`$ amortized table probe. Reverse symbol resolution ($`\text{resolve}(\sigma)`$) executes in strict $`O(1)`$ worst-case constant time via array indexing into $``\mathcal{M}_{\text{rev}}``$ with zero heap allocations. Scalar equality comparison ($``\sigma_1 == \sigma_2``$) executes in strict $`O(1)`$ constant time via a single 32-bit register comparison. Total front-end interning across $`N`$ tokens of aggregate byte size $``M = \sum |s_i|``$ is $`O(M)`$. Memory is bounded by $``O(U + N_{\text{tokens}} \cdot S_{\max})``$ where $`U`$ is unique interned byte volume, reducing AST node memory footprint by greater than 60%. String interning requires no combinatorial search, operating outside NP-complete frontiers (NP-1 through NP-8) and ensuring deterministic polynomial execution. ---
                      The classifier typing and feature usage resolution engine executes strictly within Complexity Class $`\mathbf{P}`$. Type resolution executes in $`O(1)`$ amortized time via interned symbol handles and scoped hash tables. Subtyping reachability queries ($``\tau(u) \le C_{\text{target}}``$) evaluate in $`O(1)`$ time via bitset masks or bounded tree traversal in $`O(h)`$ with inheritance depth $`h \le 32`$. Multiplicity and direction checks execute in $`O(1)`$ scalar operations. Total typing verification across $`|\mathcal{U}|`$ usages requires $`O(|\mathcal{U}| \cdot h) = O(|\mathcal{U}|)`$ linear time and $`O(|\mathcal{C}| + |\mathcal{U}|)`$ arena storage. NP Frontier Reference: While general poset subtyping can be NP-complete (NP-3, REQ-0061--REQ-0063), monotonic covariance over single/acyclic inheritance hierarchies is solvable deterministically in $`O(V + E)`$ without backtracking. ---
                      Port direction verification executes strictly within Complexity Class $`\mathbf{P}`$. Effective direction evaluation $``d_{\text{eff}}(P)``$ and connection compatibility checking execute in deterministic $`O(1)`$ constant time via direct arena offset dereferencing and 2-bit discriminant table lookup. For an AST interconnect graph $``G = (\mathcal{V}_{\text{port}}, \mathcal{E}_{\text{conn}})``$ with $``V = |\mathcal{V}_{\text{port}}|``$ ports and $``E = |\mathcal{E}_{\text{conn}}|``$ connections, total sequential verification runs in $`O(E) \subseteq O(V + E)`$ time with $`O(1)`$ auxiliary space beyond the AST memory and preallocated diagnostic sink $``O(C_{\text{max\_errors}})``$., parallel execution is bounded by $``O(E / N_{\text{max\_workers}} + \log N_{\text{max\_workers}})``$. Verification is local and acyclic; combinatorial NP frontiers (NP-1--NP-8) are bypassed with zero backtracking. ---
                      Flow payload binding and stream rate validation execute within Complexity Class $`\mathbf{P}`$. Evaluating subtyping covariance $``\tau_F :> \tau(P)``$ executes in $`O(H)`$ steps with lattice depth $`H \le 32`$, bounded by $`O(1)`$ amortized operations via bitset reachability (tractable monotonic poset subtyping avoiding general poset NP-completeness per NP-3). Serialized size lookup $``\text{sizeof}(\tau_F)``$, rate interval verification, and capacity checks execute in $`O(1)`$ scalar operations. For an AST graph with $``E = |\mathcal{E}_{\text{flow}}|``$ flows and $``K = |\mathcal{K}_{\text{channel}}|``$ channels, total verification executes in deterministic linear time $`O(E + K) \subseteq O(V + E)`$. Parallel validation on a bounded work-stealing pool with $``N_{\text{max\_workers}}``$ threads executes in $``O\left(\frac{E + K}{N_{\text{max\_workers}}} + \log N_{\text{max\_workers}}\right)``$. Auxiliary space complexity is strictly $`O(1)`$ beyond contiguous AST storage and $``O(C_{\text{max\_errors}}) = O(1)``$ preallocated diagnostic records. ---
                      The two-pass symbol resolution pipeline operates strictly within Complexity Class $`\mathbf{P}`$. 1. Pass 1 Hoisting: Traverses $``N = |\mathcal{V}_{\text{AST}}|``$ nodes once. Interning strings and inserting symbols into scope tables requires amortized $`O(1)`$ time, yielding $``T_{\text{Pass1}}(N) \in O(N)``$. 2. Pass 2 Resolution: For $``N_{\text{refs}} \le N``$ references, scope chain depth is bounded by $``D_{\text{depth}} \le 64 = O(1)``$ and qualified path lookup by $`k \le 16 = O(1)`$ segments, yielding $``T_{\text{Pass2}}(N) \in O(N)``$. 3. Bounds & Scalability: Total execution time is $`T(N) = O(N)`$ and space complexity is $`O(N)`$ arena storage with $`O(1)`$ stack depth. Parallel execution across $`K`$ packages on $``N_{\text{max\_workers}}``$ threads scales as $``O(N / N_{\text{max\_workers}} + K \log N_{\text{max\_workers}})``$. Decoupling definition hoisting from reference lookup eliminates backtracking and avoids combinatorial search, bypassing NP-hard subtyping and scheduling frontiers (NP-1 through NP-8) with guaranteed polynomial termination. ---
                      The scope resolution engine operates strictly within Complexity Class $`\mathbf{P}`$. 1. **Unqualified Lookup Complexity:** Traversal along parent pointers is bounded by lexical depth $``D_{\max} \le 16 = O(1)``$. Hash queries on interned `SymbolId` tokens require amortized $`O(1)`$ time, yielding $``T_{\text{unqual}}(\sigma) \le D_{\max} \cdot O(1) = O(1)``$. 2. **Qualified Path Resolution Complexity:** For path length $`k \le 16`$, evaluating base scope and successive member lookups requires $`O(k) = O(1)`$ time. 3. **Aggregate Bounds:** For an AST with $`N`$ references, total sequential resolution time is $``T(N) = \sum_{i=1}^N O(1) \in O(N)``$. Parallel execution across units on $``N_{\text{max\_workers}}``$ threads scales as $``O(N / N_{\text{max\_workers}} + |\mathcal{S}| \log N_{\text{max\_workers}})``$. Space complexity is $`O(N)`$ arena storage with $`O(1)`$ auxiliary stack frames. Bounded scope depth and direct hash lookups bypass backtracking, avoiding NP-hard subtyping and scheduling frontiers (NP-1 through NP-8) with guaranteed polynomial termination. ---
                      Cycle verification operates strictly within Complexity Class $`\mathbf{P}`$. 1. Direct Arena Indexing: Auxiliary DFS state arrays (`dfn`, `lowlink`, `on_stack`) are indexed directly by 32-bit arena offset in $`O(1)`$ constant time. 2. Linear Graph Traversal: In directed graph $`G = (\mathcal{V}, \mathcal{E})`$ with $`V = |\mathcal{V}|`$ nodes and $`E = |\mathcal{E}|`$ edges, Tarjan's algorithm inspects every node and edge exactly once: T_{\text{SCC}}(V, E) \in O(V + E) 3. Space & Parallel Bounds: Auxiliary memory is bounded by $`O(V)`$ for DFS arrays and stack $``\mathcal{S}_{\text{stack}}``$. Parallel execution across $`M`$ components over $``N_{\text{max\_workers}}``$ threads scales as $``O((V + E) / N_{\text{max\_workers}} + M \log N_{\text{max\_workers}})``$. Operating strictly on directed graphs via linear DFS numbering eliminates backtracking, bypassing NP-hard subtyping and scheduling frontiers (NP-1 through NP-8) and ensuring deterministic polynomial termination. ---
                      Multi-file module ingestion operates strictly within Complexity Class $`\mathbf{P}`$. For $``M = |\mathcal{F}_{\text{comp}}|``$ files with aggregate path byte length $``L = \sum_{f} |P_{\text{rel}}(f)|``$: 1. **Path Normalization**: Decomposing and PascalCase-normalizing segments runs in deterministic linear time $`O(L)`$ across the workspace. 2. **Package Lookup & Synthesis**: Scoped lookup and arena allocation execute in $`O(1)`$ amortized time per level via interned `SymbolId` handles, bounded by $``O(D_{\max}) = O(1)``$ per file. 3. **Aggregate Complexity**: Constructing the namespace forest executes in $`O(L + M) \subseteq O(V + E)`$ time and requires $`O(V + E)`$ space for AST nodes and containment edges. The pass requires zero combinatorial backtracking, operating strictly below NP frontiers (e.g., NP-3 subtyping lattice resolution). ---
                      The serialization and deserialization engine operates within Complexity Class $`\mathbf{P}`$. Traversing $`N = |\mathcal{V}|`$ nodes, $`E = |\mathcal{E}|`$ edges, and $``M = |\mathcal{T}_{\text{sym}}|``$ interned symbols executes in deterministic linear time $`O(N + E + M) = O(N)`$. Reconstructing node slots into contiguous bump arena storage takes $`O(1)`$ amortized time per node, and symbol interning requires $`O(1)`$ amortized time per string, yielding total deserialization complexity $`O(|\mathcal{B}|) = O(N)`$ for input byte stream $`\mathcal{B}`$. In compiled Release mode, CBOR deserialization of a 100,000-node model completes in under 15 ms. The operations operate outside NP-hard frontiers (e.g., NP-1 through NP-8) without combinatorial search or backtracking. Auxiliary space is bounded by $`O(N)`$ for serialization byte buffers and $`O(1)`$ stack space. ---
                      The immutable graph traversal engine executes within Complexity Class $`\mathbf{P}`$. Traversal over reachable subgraphs runs in deterministic linear time $`O(|V| + |E|)`$ for reachable vertices $``V = |V_{\text{reach}}(v_0)|``$ and edges $``E = |E_{\text{reach}}(v_0)|``$. Direct arena handle dereferences and bitset lookups execute in $`O(1)`$ constant time via base-plus-offset addressing and bitwise masks. Auxiliary workspace memory is bounded by $``O(C_{\text{arena}})``$ bits for the bitset ($``\lceil C_{\text{arena}} / 8 \rceil``$ bytes) and $``O(D_{\text{max\_depth}})``$ for the frontier stack/queue, incurring zero heap allocations ($`\text{HeapAllocs} = 0`$)., parallel time satisfies $``O((|V| + |E|) / N_{\text{max\_workers}} + \log N_{\text{max\_workers}})``$. Traversal accesses pre-constructed edges directly, bypassing combinatorial graph search NP frontiers (NP-1--NP-8) without backtracking. ---
                      The mutable AST transformation and in-place rewrite engine operates strictly within Complexity Class $`\mathbf{P}`$. 1. In-Place Mutation: Strict $`O(1)`$ worst-case constant time via array base-plus-offset addressing ($``\text{Base} + i \cdot S_{\text{slot}}``$), bitwise update, and modular generation increment. 2. Handle Validation: Strict $`O(1)`$ worst-case constant time via bounds check ($`i \lt C`$), generation match ($``g_t(i) = g_H``$), and tag match ($``\tau_t(i) = \tau_H``$). 3. Full Rewrite Pass: Deterministic $`O(V)`$ linear time for attribute modifications and $`O(V + E)`$ linear time for structural rewrites, where $`V = |\text{Nodes}(\mathcal{A})|`$ and $`E = |\text{Edges}(\mathcal{A})|`$. 4. Parallel Bounds: Processing $`K`$ subtrees on $``N_{\text{max\_workers}}``$ threads scales as $``O(\lceil K / N_{\text{max\_workers}} \rceil \cdot (V_{\text{unit}} + E_{\text{unit}}))``$. 5. Space Complexity: Strict $`O(1)`$ auxiliary memory for in-place updates, recursion depth $``D_{\text{max}} \le 64 = O(1)``$, and $`O(V)`$ arena storage with zero dynamic heap allocations. Local in-place AST rewrites execute deterministically without backtracking or state-space explosion, bypassing combinatorial NP-hard frontiers (e.g., NP-1, NP-3, NP-7) with guaranteed polynomial-time termination. ---
                      The AST multigraph operates strictly within Complexity Class $`\mathbf{P}`$. - Node Access: Vertex lookup executes in worst-case $`O(1)`$ time via arena indexing. - Edge Insertion: Inserting directed edge $`e = (u, v, k, \mathbf{p})`$ executes in amortized $`O(1)`$ time, yielding $`O(|V| + |E|)`$ total construction time for $`|V|`$ vertices and $`|E|`$ edges. - Neighbor Retrieval: Forward queries $`\text{OutEdges}(v)`$ and reverse queries $`\text{InEdges}(v)`$ execute in output-sensitive $`O(\text{deg}(v))`$ time proportional to local degree $`\text{deg}(v) = |\text{OutEdges}(v)| + |\text{InEdges}(v)|`$. Kind filtering evaluates within $`O(\text{deg}(v))`$ time. - Space Complexity: Memory scales linearly in $`O(|V| + |E|)`$ space using compact 32-bit index slabs without pointer indirection. Deterministic adjacency indexing eliminates combinatorial backtracking, bypassing NP-hard register allocation (NP-7) and state reachability (NP-5) frontiers with guaranteed polynomial termination. ---
                      Topological scheduling, Tarjan SCC decomposition, and shortest-path routing execute strictly within Complexity Class $`\mathbf{P}`$. - Kahn Topological Scheduling: $``T_{\text{Kahn}}(V, E) \in O(|V| \log |V| + |E|)``$ time and $`O(|V|)`$ space using a min-heap with $`O(1)`$ scalar comparisons on interned symbols. - Tarjan SCC Decomposition: Optimal linear time $``T_{\text{Tarjan}}(V, E) \in O(|V| + |E|)``$ and $`O(|V|)`$ stack space. - Condensation DAG Construction: Executes in $`O(|V| + |E|)`$ time and space. - BFS Shortest-Path Queries: Traverses interconnect multigraphs in $`O(|V| + |E|)`$ time and $`O(|V|)`$ queue memory. - Parallel Scheduling: Across $`K`$ components on $``N_{\text{max\_workers}}``$ threads scales as $``O((|V| + |E|) / N_{\text{max\_workers}} + K \log N_{\text{max\_workers}})``$. Linear-time decomposition and tie-breaking eliminate backtracking, bypassing NP-complete subtyping (NP-3) and state reachability (NP-5) frontiers with guaranteed polynomial termination. ---
                      Context-bounded AST slice extraction executes strictly within Complexity Class $`\mathbf{P}`$: - **Target Handle Dereferencing**: Generational validation executes in strict $`O(1)`$ constant time with zero heap allocations. - **Bounded Breadth-First Search**: For ball vertex count $``V_k = |\mathcal{B}_k(n_{\text{target}})|``$ and incident edges $``E_k``$, traversal executes in $``O(V_k + E_k)``$ time. - **Transitive Semantic Closure**: Closure evaluates in $``O(|V_{\text{slice}}|)``$ time via $`O(1)`$ arena lookups per referenced handle. - **Total Slice Extraction**: Total execution time satisfies $``O(|V_{\text{slice}}| + |E_{\text{slice}}|)``$ with auxiliary space bounded by $``O(|V_{\text{slice}}|)``$ queue and bitset storage. - **Bounded Work-Stealing Concurrency**: Parallel dispatch across $``N_{\text{max\_workers}}``$ threads scales as $``O((|V_{\text{slice}}| + |E_{\text{slice}}|) / N_{\text{max\_workers}} + \log N_{\text{max\_workers}})``$. - **NP-Frontier Avoidance**: Bounded slicing operates strictly in polynomial time without combinatorial search, avoiding NP-complete subtyping (NP-3) and state reachability (NP-5). ---
                      The slice extraction, dependency envelope reification, and serialization pipeline executes strictly within Complexity Class $`\mathbf{P}`$. Scanning symbol references across the slice subgraph executes in $``O(|V_{\text{slice}}| \cdot \bar{d})``$ worst-case time, where $`\bar{d} = O(1)`$ is the average reference fan-out degree bounded by metamodel typing limits. Minimal stub extraction and envelope construction execute in $``O(|V_{\text{deps}}|)``$ time via amortized $`O(1)`$ symbol table lookups. Handle remapping and linear buffer emission (binary or JSON) execute in deterministic linear time $``O(|V_{\text{slice}}| + |V_{\text{deps}}| + |E_{\text{slice}}| + |E_{\text{deps}}|)``$. Auxiliary working memory is bounded by $``O(|V_{\text{deps}}|)``$ for resolution sets and $``O(C_{\text{max\_errors}}) = O(1)``$ for the preallocated diagnostic sink. Bounded polynomial execution guarantees total-function termination, avoiding combinatorial search and bypassing the NP-hard frontiers of subtyping posets (NP-3) and channel allocation (NP-2). ---
                      The module dependency tracking and incremental cache invalidation engine operates strictly within Complexity Class $`\mathbf{P}`$. DAG construction, cycle detection, and topological sorting execute in deterministic linear time $`O(|V| + |E|)`$ and auxiliary memory $`O(|V| + |E|)`$ via Tarjan's SCC or Kahn's algorithm. State hash propagation in topological order requires $`O(|V| + |E|)`$ aggregate time. Transitive invalidation closure $`\mathcal{R}`$ computation executes in $``O(|\mathcal{R}| + |E_{\mathcal{R}}|) \subseteq O(|V| + |E|)``$ via forward breadth-first search. Cache lookup for unimpacted modules $`V \setminus \mathcal{R}`$ requires $`O(1)`$ amortized time per unit. Incremental invalidation operates strictly in polynomial time $`\mathbf{P}`$, requiring no combinatorial search and bypassing downstream NP-hard frontiers such as NP-2 (REQ-0147) and NP-7 (REQ-0180). ---
                      The memory-mapped diff engine operates within Complexity Class $`\mathbf{P}`$: - **Metadata Query**: Inspection of file existence and size executes in $`O(1)`$ constant time via filesystem inode caching. - **Short-Circuit Elimination**: When $``\text{FileSize}(P) \ne |B_{\text{new}}|``$, evaluation terminates in $`O(1)`$ time without memory mapping or disk I/O. - **Byte Comparison**: For matching sizes $``L = |B_{\text{new}}|``$, vectorized word comparison evaluates $`\lceil L / W \rceil`$ iterations ($`W = 32\text{ bytes}`$ under SIMD), executing in $`O(k^*)`$ time where $`k^* \le L`$ is the first divergent byte (worst-case $`O(L)`$ when identical). - **Parallel Scaling**: Across $`K`$ artifacts distributed over $``N_{\text{max\_workers}}``$ threads, wall-clock persistence time is bounded by $``O(\lceil K / N_{\text{max\_workers}} \rceil \cdot \bar{L})``$. - **Space Bounds**: Auxiliary heap memory is strictly $`O(1)`$. - **NP-Frontier Avoidance**: Diffing evaluates strictly linear byte sequences, bypassing combinatorial NP frontiers (NP-1 through NP-8). ---
                      The tombstone pruning engine executes strictly within Complexity Class $`\mathbf{P}`$. For $``N_{\text{prev}} = |\mathcal{A}_{\text{manifest}}|``$ and $``N_{\text{act}} = |\mathcal{A}_{\text{active}}|``$, hash set insertion and set difference evaluate in $``O(N_{\text{prev}} + N_{\text{act}})``$ linear time. Unlinking $``K = |\mathcal{T}_{\text{prune}}|``$ files executes in $`O(K)`$ filesystem operations, and empty directory cleanup ascends bounded depth $``D_{\max} \le 32``$ in $``O(K \cdot D_{\max})``$ steps. Total sequential runtime satisfies: T_{\text{total}} \in O(N_{\text{prev}} + N_{\text{act}} + K \cdot D_{\max}) \subseteq O(N_{\text{prev}} + N_{\text{act}}) Parallel execution over $``N_{\text{max\_workers}}``$ threads scales as $``O((N_{\text{prev}} + N_{\text{act}}) / N_{\text{max\_workers}} + \log N_{\text{max\_workers}})``$. Memory footprint is bounded by $``O((N_{\text{prev}} + N_{\text{act}}) \cdot \bar{L}_{\text{path}})``$, with stack depth bounded by $``O(D_{\max}) \le 32``$ frames. NP Frontier Reference: Decoupled from NP-hard combinatorial frontiers (NP-1 through NP-8); all set operations and directory traversals evaluate deterministically in polynomial time without backtracking. ---
                      KerML root abstract syntax lowering, relationship endpoint validation, and containment cycle checking execute strictly within Complexity Class $`\mathbf{P}`$. Contiguous AST memory slot allocation and handle dereferencing execute in deterministic $`O(1)`$ constant time with zero pointer indirection. Endpoint validation for bounded endpoints ($``K_{\text{src}}, K_{\text{tgt}} \le 16``$) requires $`O(1)`$ per relationship, yielding $`O(|\mathcal{R}|)`$ total endpoint validation time. Single ownership verification requires $`O(1)`$ time per element, bounding model checks to $`O(|\mathcal{E}|)`$. Tarjan strongly connected components (SCC) cycle detection on containment forest $``G_{\text{own}} = (\mathcal{E}, \mathcal{O})``$ evaluates in deterministic linear time $`O(|\mathcal{E}| + |\mathcal{O}|) \subseteq O(|\mathcal{E}|)`$ since $`|\mathcal{O}| \le |\mathcal{E}|`$. Annotation attachment and lookup execute in $`O(1)`$ amortized and $``O(|\mathcal{A}_e|)``$ linear time. Parallel lowering over $``N_{\text{max\_workers}}``$ threads scales as $``O((|\mathcal{E}| + |\mathcal{R}| + |\mathcal{M}|) / N_{\text{max\_workers}} + \log N_{\text{max\_workers}})``$. Arena memory footprint is bounded by $`O(|\mathcal{E}| + |\mathcal{R}| + |\mathcal{M}|)`$, with stack depth bounded by $``O(D_{\max}) \le 64``$ frames. NP Frontier Reference: Decoupled from NP-hard combinatorial frontiers (NP-1 through NP-8); all root structural graph checks evaluate deterministically in linear time $`O(V + E)`$ without backtracking. ---
                      Namespace indexing and resolution execute strictly within Complexity Class $`\mathbf{P}`$. Local symbol lookup executes in amortized $`O(1)`$ time via interned 32-bit `SymbolId` handles. Ownership tree acyclicity verification executes in deterministic linear time $``O(|\mathcal{E}| + |\mathcal{M}_{\text{own}}|) \subseteq O(|\mathcal{E}|)``$ via depth-first cycle detection. Qualified name construction traverses bounded depth $``D_{\max} \le 16``$ in bounded constant time $``O(D_{\max}) = O(1)``$. Qualified path resolution of length $`k \le 16`$ executes $`k`$ sequential lookups in $`O(k) = O(1)`$ time. Total compilation runtime satisfies $``T_{\text{total}} \in O(V + M + N_{\text{ref}})``$. Parallel execution over $``N_{\text{max\_workers}}``$ threads scales as $``O((V + M) / N_{\text{max\_workers}} + \log N_{\text{max\_workers}})``$. Arena memory is bounded by $`O(V + M)`$, with stack depth bounded by $``O(D_{\max}) \le 16``$ frames ($`O(1)`$ stack space). NP Frontier Reference: Decoupled from NP-hard combinatorial frontiers (NP-1 through NP-8); all namespace scoping and naming recurrences evaluate deterministically in linear polynomial time without backtracking. ---
                      The KerML import declaration evaluation and scoping engine executes strictly within Complexity Class $`\mathbf{P}`$. For a model with $`V`$ namespaces, $`E`$ containment edges, and $`M`$ memberships, hierarchy traversal evaluates via depth-first search in $`O(V + E)`$ time. Direct and wildcard import set constructions execute in $`O(M)`$ operations over indexed membership hash sets. Unqualified symbol resolution against hash-indexed scopes executes in $`O(1)`$ amortized time ($`O(L)`$ for identifier length $`L`$). Total runtime satisfies: T_{\text{import}} \in O(V + E + M \cdot L) \subseteq O(V + E + M) Memory footprint is bounded by $`O(V + M)`$ references in contiguous storage. NP Frontier Reference: Decoupled from NP-complete multiple inheritance and subtyping lattice frontiers (NP-3, REQ-0061--REQ-0063); import resolution and shadow precedence evaluate deterministically in linear polynomial time without combinatorial search or backtracking. ---
                      Core classifier lowering, subtyping verification, and cycle detection execute within Complexity Class $`\mathbf{P}`$. For $`V = |\mathcal{K}|`$ classifiers and $``E = |\mathcal{E}_{\text{gen}}|``$ generalization edges: - Arena node allocation and symbol interning take amortized $`O(1)`$ time per classifier, giving $`O(V)`$ initialization. - Acyclicity validation via Tarjan's SCC algorithm executes in deterministic $`O(V + E)`$ time and $`O(V)`$ space. - Single-inheritance subtyping queries execute in $`O(1)`$ time via precomputed interval containment timestamps ($``l_{C_2} \le l_{C_1} \wedge r_{C_1} \le r_{C_2}``$). - Multiple-inheritance reachability queries execute in $`O(V + E)`$ time, or $`O(1)`$ query time following $`O(V(V + E))`$ transitive closure bitset precomputation. This grounds the tractable subclass of NP-3 (Subtyping Lattice & C3 Linearization), where subtyping over the monotonic meet-semilattice is resolved in deterministic $`O(V + E)`$ time without backtracking. ---
                      The association lowering and link verification engine executes strictly within Complexity Class $`\mathbf{P}`$. For $`|A|`$ associations, $`M = |⟦ A ⟧|`$ links, and $`N`$ participant instances: - Association arity and definition validation execute in $`O(|A| \cdot k)`$ time for bounded arity $`k \le 16`$. - Link participant type checking evaluates in $`O(M \cdot k) = O(M)`$ time. - Fiber multiplicity bound verification executes in $`O(M + N)`$ time via indexed hashing. Total sequential runtime satisfies: T_{\text{total}} \in O(|A| + M + N) Parallel execution over $``N_{\text{max\_workers}}``$ threads scales as $``O((|A| + M + N) / N_{\text{max\_workers}} + \log N_{\text{max\_workers}})``$. Memory footprint is bounded by $`O((|A| + M) \cdot k)`$ in the AST. NP Frontier Reference: Decoupled from NP-hard subtyping posets (NP-3); participant conformance checks evaluate against single-inheritance chains or precomputed C3 linearizations in deterministic $`O(1)`$ time without backtracking. ---
                      Semantic lowering and precedence verification execute strictly within Complexity Class $`\mathbf{P}`$. For interaction $`I`$ with $``V = |\mathcal{S}_{I}|``$ steps, $``E = |\prec_{I}|``$ precedence edges, and $``K = |\mathcal{P}_{I}|``$ participant ends: 1. Participant and Step Validation: Checking cardinality $`K \ge 2`$, step registrations, and message timestamp ordering $``\tau(e_{\text{send}}) \lt \tau(e_{\text{recv}})``$ executes in deterministic $`O(K + V)`$ time. 2. Subtyping Conformance: Validating payload typing $``\tau(\delta_{\text{payload}}) \le_{\text{sub}} \tau_{\text{msg}}``$ against the subtyping poset executes in $`O(1)`$ time per step via precomputed tables, resolving C3 linearization without backtracking in deterministic $`O(V + E)`$ time per NP-3. 3. Precedence Graph Acyclicity: Constructing adjacency structures and executing topological sorting via in-degree reduction or Tarjan strongly connected components processes vertices and edges in $`O(V + E)`$ time, isolating cycles in $`O(V + E)`$ time. 4. Total Asymptotic Bound: Aggregate sequential execution time is strictly linear: T_{\text{total}}(V, E, K) \in O(K + V + E) \subseteq O(V + E) Auxiliary memory is bounded by $`O(V + E)`$ for adjacency and in-degree structures, with zero unbounded heap allocations, bypassing NP-hard frontiers and guaranteeing deterministic polynomial termination. ---
                      The feature, parameter, step, and typing lowering pass operates within Complexity Class $`\mathbf{P}`$. AST node construction and symbol interning execute in amortized $`O(1)`$ time and $`O(1)`$ space. Multiplicity checks, fiber cardinality evaluation, and parameter direction validation require strictly $`O(1)`$ scalar operations. For an AST with $``N_{\text{feat}}``$ features and typing hierarchy depth $`h \le 32 = O(1)`$, total verification time is $``O(N_{\text{feat}})``$ with $`O(1)`$ auxiliary memory. While general multiple-inheritance subtyping posets can induce NP-complete verification (NP-3), single-step FeatureTyping conformance queries execute over precomputed monotonic lattices in deterministic $`O(1)`$ time per feature. ---
                      Feature subsetting lowering and subtyping verification execute within Complexity Class $`\mathbf{P}`$. While general subtyping satisfiability in arbitrary posets is NP-complete (**NP-3**), restricting classifier inheritance to monotonic meet-semilattices $`(\mathcal{T}, \le, \wedge)`$ with deterministic C3 linearization reduces subtyping reachability and subsetting validation to deterministic $`O(V + E)`$ time without backtracking, where $`V = |\mathcal{F}|`$ features and $``E = |\mathcal{R}_{\text{subset}}|``$ edges. Cycle detection via Tarjan strongly connected components and scalar multiplicity checks execute in $`O(V + E)`$ time and $`O(V + E)`$ auxiliary memory. ---
                      The redefinition lowering pass executes within Complexity Class $`\mathbf{P}`$. In unconstrained posets, subtyping satisfiability and multiple-inheritance redefinition resolution is NP-complete (NP-3). Constraining type hierarchies to a monotonic meet-semilattice and resolving inheritance via deterministic C3 linearization without backtracking guarantees polynomial tractability: - C3 linearization executes in $`O(V + E)`$ time, where $`V = |\mathcal{C}|`$ and $`E`$ is the number of specialization edges. - Subtyping conformance and multiplicity checks evaluate in $`O(1)`$ amortized time per edge via reachability bitsets and bounded inheritance depth $`h \le 32`$. - Redefinition cycle detection evaluates in $`O(V + E)`$ time via Tarjan's SCC algorithm. - Total verification time across $``N_f``$ features and $``E_{\text{redef}}``$ redefinition edges is strictly $``O(V + E + N_f + E_{\text{redef}})``$, guaranteeing release latency $`< 25\text{ ms}`$ and memory $`< 100\text{ MB}`$ RSS. ---
                      Port and interface conjugation executes strictly within Complexity Class $`\mathbf{P}`$. For classifier $`P`$ declaring $`K = |\text{features}(P)|`$ features, inverted feature synthesis executes in deterministic linear time $`O(K) = O(|\text{features}(P)|)`$ with zero memory reallocation beyond contiguous AST storage. Operator normalization $`{\sim}^k`$ executes in $`O(1)`$ scalar parity arithmetic. Multiple-inheritance subtyping posets under conjugation are restricted to a monotonic meet-semilattice and linearized via C3 in deterministic $`O(V + E)`$ polynomial time, eliminating exponential search and backtracking (Algorithmic Bound NP-3). Auxiliary memory is bounded by $`O(1)`$ stack space. ---
                      Type algebra evaluation and disjointness verification execute in Complexity Class $`\mathbf{P}`$. For $`V = |\mathcal{T}|`$ classifier types and $`E`$ specialization and disjointness edges: 1. Poset & Disjointness Queries: Subtyping reachability bitset lookup executes in $`O(1)`$ time following $`O(V + E)`$ transitive closure initialization. 2. Compound Type Normalization: Set-theoretic operations over finite bitsets evaluate in deterministic $`O(V)`$ time. 3. Model Invariant Checking: Complete model disjointness verification executes in $`O(V \cdot \lceil V / 64 \rceil) \subseteq O(V^2)`$ worst-case and $`O(V + E)`$ sparse time. 4. Parallel Work-Stealing: Scheduled on $``N_{\text{max\_workers}}``$ threads, verification executes in $``O((V + E) / N_{\text{max\_workers}} + \log N_{\text{max\_workers}})``$ time. 5. Space Bounds: Storage is bounded by $`O(V + E)`$ arena memory and $``O(C_{\text{max\_errors}}) = O(1)``$ error sink buffer. NP Frontier Reference (NP-3): Arbitrary poset subtyping with Boolean operators is NP-complete. Restricting type algebra expressions to finite classifier meet-semilattices and deterministic C3 linearization without backtracking guarantees deterministic polynomial-time execution. ---
                      The expression lowering and type checking pipeline operates strictly within Complexity Class $`\mathbf{P}`$. Contiguous arena node allocation and symbol interning execute in amortized $`O(1)`$ time and $`O(1)`$ space. Bottom-up post-order type inference traverses expression trees in deterministic linear time $`O(N)`$ for $``N = |\mathcal{E}_{\text{expr}}|``$ nodes, performing $`O(1)`$ signature and subtyping lookups per node. Constant expression cycle detection executes in $``O(N_{\text{const}} + E_{\text{const}}) \subseteq O(N)``$ via Tarjan SCC. While subtyping in arbitrary posets is NP-complete (NP-3), restricting expressions to bounded meet-semilattices with C3 linearization guarantees deterministic $`O(N)`$ execution without backtracking. ---
                      Function lowering, signature verification, and static type checking execute within Complexity Class $`\mathbf{P}`$. For $`M`$ functions with $`K`$ parameters, $`N`$ expression nodes, and call graph $``G_{\text{call}} = (V, E)``$: - Arena allocation and symbol interning take amortized $`O(1)`$ per node, yielding $`O(M + K + N)`$ initialization. - Parameter directionality and multiplicity validation execute in $`O(K)`$ time. - Body expression type inference and subtyping conformance execute in $`O(N)`$ time. Subtyping checks $``T_{\text{eval}} \le T_{\text{ret}}``$ execute in $`O(1)`$ time via precomputed reachability bitsets ($`h \le 32`$), grounding the tractable subclass of NP-3 (Subtyping Lattice & C3 Linearization) without backtracking. - Invocation checking executes in $`O(k)`$ for $`k`$ arguments. - Call graph cycle detection executes in deterministic $`O(V + E)`$ time via Tarjan's SCC algorithm. - Pure non-recursive expressions evaluate with strictly $`O(1)`$ stack overhead. Total sequential complexity is $`O(M + K + N + V + E)`$. Parallel validation on a work-stealing pool scales as $``O((M + K + N + V + E) / N_{\text{max\_workers}} + \log N_{\text{max\_workers}})``$. ---
                      The occurrence lowering, typing, and temporal containment validation passes execute strictly within Complexity Class $\mathbf{P}$. For an input model with $``V = |\mathcal{O}_{\text{all}}|``$ occurrence nodes and $``E = |\mathcal{R}_{\text{sub\_occ}}|``$ containment edges: 1. Node allocation, interning, and type tag checking execute in amortized $O(1)$ time per node, totaling $O(V)$ time. 2. Temporal bounds validation $``t_{\text{start}} \le t_{\text{end}}``$ and multiplicity checking $``m_{\min} \le m_{\max}``$ execute in $O(1)$ scalar comparisons per node, totaling $O(V)$ time. 3. Sub-occurrence temporal containment checking requires $O(1)$ scalar comparisons per edge, totaling $O(E)$ time. 4. Sub-occurrence acyclicity checking executes in $O(V + E)$ time and $O(V)$ space via Tarjan's strongly connected components algorithm. Total sequential verification complexity is $O(V + E)$. While unconstrained temporal constraint satisfiability is NP-complete (NP-1, REQ-0117), static occurrence temporal checking in REQ-0067 evaluates fixed scalar bounds over hierarchical containment DAGs without backtracking. Parallel package execution scales as $``O((V + E) / N_{\text{max\_workers}} + \log N_{\text{max\_workers}})``$. ---
                      The portion lowering, temporal validation, and predicate typing passes execute strictly within Complexity Class $\mathbf{P}$. For an input model with $N$ portion nodes: 1. Node allocation in AST memory and symbol interning take amortized $O(1)$ time per node, totaling $O(N)$ time. 2. Temporal lifetime verification ($``t_{\text{start}} \le t_0 \le t_{\text{end}}``$) executes in strictly 2 scalar comparisons ($O(1)$ time). 3. Time slice interval validation ($``t_1 \le t_2``$ and parent containment) executes in strictly 3 scalar comparisons ($O(1)$ time). 4. Trigger predicate boolean type check evaluates root type tag in $O(1)$ time. Total sequential verification complexity is $O(N)$. Because all temporal bounds are fixed scalar intervals without existential quantification or backtracking, this pass operates strictly within the tractable polynomial fragment of NP-1 (Temporal Constraint Satisfiability). Parallel execution across packages scales as $``O(N / N_{\text{max\_workers}} + \log N_{\text{max\_workers}})``$. ---
                      The behavior, step, and performance lowering passes execute strictly within Complexity Class $\mathbf{P}$. For a behavioral model with $V$ step nodes and $E$ precedence edges: 1. Node allocation in typed AST memory and symbol interning execute in amortized $O(1)$ time per node, totaling $O(V)$ time. 2. Parameter directionality and multiplicity checking execute in $O(K)$ time for $K$ parameters. 3. Precomputed monotonic subtyping lattice queries execute in $O(1)$ time per argument, grounding the tractable polynomial fragment of NP-3 (Subtyping Lattice & C3 Linearization). 4. Sub-step temporal containment and precedence verification execute in $O(V + E)$ time without backtracking. Total sequential verification complexity is $O(V + E)$. Parallel package execution scales as $``O((V + E) / N_{\text{max\_workers}} + \log N_{\text{max\_workers}})``$. ---
                      The package organization, import resolution, and cycle validation passes execute strictly within Complexity Class $\mathbf{P}$. For a package graph with $V = |\mathcal{P}|$ package nodes and $``E = |\mathcal{E}_{\text{dep}}|``$ dependency edges: 1. AST node construction and symbol interning execute in amortized $O(1)$ time per node. 2. Visibility checking and encapsulation filtering execute in $O(1)$ scalar checks per symbol reference. 3. Package graph acyclicity validation executes in $O(V + E)$ time via Tarjan's strongly connected components or Kahn's topological sort. 4. Wildcard and alias import bindings resolve in $O(S)$ time for $S$ exported symbols without backtracking. Total sequential verification complexity is $O(V + E)$. Parallel package processing scales as $``O((V + E) / N_{\text{max\_workers}} + \log N_{\text{max\_workers}})``$. ---
                      The part definition lowering, typing, and containment validation passes execute strictly within Complexity Class $\mathbf{P}$. For an input model with $``V = |\mathcal{D}_{\text{part}}|``$ part definition nodes and $``E = |\mathcal{E}_{\text{contain}}|``$ containment edges: 1. Node allocation in AST memory and symbol interning execute in amortized $O(1)$ time per node, totaling $O(V)$ time. 2. Multiplicity validation $``m_{\min} \le m_{\max}``$ executes in $O(1)$ scalar comparisons per usage. 3. Subtyping conformance against the precomputed KerML lattice evaluates in $O(1)$ time per part usage, grounding the tractable fragment of NP-3. 4. Sub-assembly containment acyclicity checking executes in $O(V + E)$ time and $O(V)$ space via Tarjan's strongly connected components algorithm. Total sequential verification complexity is $O(V + E)$. Parallel package execution scales as $``O((V + E) / N_{\text{max\_workers}} + \log N_{\text{max\_workers}})``$. ---
                      The port definition lowering, typing, and direction inversion passes execute strictly within Complexity Class $\mathbf{P}$. For an input model with $N$ port usages and $F$ total interface features: 1. Node allocation in typed arena and symbol interning execute in amortized $O(1)$ time per node, totaling $O(N)$ time. 2. Port type resolution evaluates via symbol table lookup in $O(1)$ time. 3. Conjugate interface expansion and feature direction inversion evaluate in $O(F)$ time, linear in the number of interface features without combinatorial search. 4. Boundary encapsulation validation executes in $O(1)$ set membership checks per connection endpoint. Total sequential verification complexity is $O(N + F)$. Parallel package lowering scales as $``O((N + F) / N_{\text{max\_workers}} + \log N_{\text{max\_workers}})``$. ---
                      The item definition lowering, typing, and flow balance checking passes execute strictly within Complexity Class $\mathbf{P}$. For an input model with $N$ item usages and $F$ flow connections: 1. Node allocation in typed arena and symbol interning execute in amortized $O(1)$ time per node, totaling $O(N)$ time. 2. Duality classification (value vs. token) validates in deterministic $O(1)$ subtyping checks against the precomputed lattice. 3. Flow conservation equations evaluate in $O(F)$ linear time by aggregating incoming and outgoing rates at junction nodes. 4. Multiplicity and token interval checks evaluate in $O(1)$ scalar comparisons per usage. Total sequential verification complexity is $O(N + F)$. Parallel package lowering scales as $``O((N + F) / N_{\text{max\_workers}} + \log N_{\text{max\_workers}})``$. ---
                      The connection definition lowering, endpoint resolution, and compatibility checking passes execute strictly within Complexity Class $\mathbf{P}$. For an input model with $E$ connection usages and $V$ port endpoints: 1. Endpoint resolution in hierarchical symbol tables executes in $O(1)$ amortized time per endpoint, totaling $O(E)$ time. 2. Endpoint type conformance checking against the conjugate type lattice evaluates in $O(1)$ time per connection, grounding the polynomial fragment of NP-3. 3. Directional flow compatibility checks execute in strictly $O(1)$ scalar comparisons per connector. 4. Topological scope visibility validation verifies lowest common ancestor containment in $O(D)$ time where tree depth $D \le 16$ (REQ-0070), bounded by $O(1)$ constant time. Total sequential verification complexity is $O(E + V)$. Parallel package lowering scales as $``O((E + V) / N_{\text{max\_workers}} + \log N_{\text{max\_workers}})``$. ---
                      Interface lowering and verification execute within Complexity Class $\mathbf{P}$. For an AST model with $M$ interface definitions, $N$ interface usages, $F$ features, and $E$ specialization edges: - **Dual End-Role Conjugation**: $O(1)$ nominal type comparisons per definition ($O(M)$ total). - **Port Binding Subtyping**: $O(1)$ amortized check per usage via cached bitsets ($O(N)$ total). - **Flow Payload Conformance**: $O(1)$ check per declared flow feature ($O(F)$ total). - **C3 Linearization**: Resolves the monotonic meet-semilattice hierarchy (tractable fragment of NP-3) in $O(V + E)$ time without backtracking. Total sequential complexity is $O(M + N + F + E)$. Bounded work-stealing parallel lowering scales as $``O((M + N + F + E) / N_{\text{max\_workers}} + \log N_{\text{max\_workers}})``$. ---
                      Item flow lowering executes strictly within Complexity Class $\mathbf{P}$. For $V$ ports, $E$ connectors, and $F$ item flows: 1. Endpoint resolution operates in $O(1)$ amortized time ($O(F)$ total). 2. Directional compatibility checks evaluate 2-bit direction flags in $O(1)$ time per flow. 3. Payload subtyping covariance evaluates in $O(1)$ time against the precomputed meet-semilattice (tractable fragment of NP-3). 4. Rate interval validation and bandwidth comparisons evaluate in $O(1)$ scalar operations. Total verification complexity is $O(V + E + F)$. Parallel package lowering scales as $``O((V + E + F) / N_{\text{max\_workers}} + \log N_{\text{max\_workers}})``$. ---
                      Allocation lowering, bipartite validation, port consistency, and acyclicity passes execute in Complexity Class $\mathbf{P}$: - Record allocation, symbol interning, and bipartite tagging execute in $O(1)$ amortized time per element, totaling $O(V + E)$ for $``V = |V_S| + |V_T|``$ elements and $``E = |E_{\text{alloc}}|``$ allocations. - Interface port consistency evaluates in $O(1)$ time per edge given bounded port count ($``K_{\text{ports}} \le 64``$) and subtyping bitsets, totaling $O(E)$. - Acyclicity verification over $``(V, E_{\text{alloc}})``$ executes via Tarjan SCC in deterministic $O(V + E)$ time and $O(V)$ space. **Tractable Subclass & Frontier**: While general allocation is NP-hard (governed by NP-2 in REQ-0147), static allocation lowering operates over declared relations in deterministic $O(V + E)$ time without backtracking. Parallel lowering scales as $``O((V + E) / N_{\text{max\_workers}} + \log N_{\text{max\_workers}})``$. ---
                      Action lowering and verification execute strictly within Complexity Class $\mathbf{P}$. For action definition with $``V = |V_{\text{steps}}| + |V_{\text{ctrl}}|``$ nodes and $``E = |E_{\text{ctrl}}| + |E_{\text{data}}|``$ edges: - Node allocation and symbol interning execute in amortized $O(1)$ time per element ($O(V)$). - Parameter directionality and subtyping evaluate in $O(1)$ checks via precomputed bitsets (NP-3 fragment). - Control degree checking traverses incident edges in $``O(|V_{\text{ctrl}}| + |E_{\text{ctrl}}|)``$ time. - Dependency acyclicity evaluates in deterministic $O(V + E)$ time using Kahn's algorithm or Tarjan's SCC. Total sequential complexity is $O(V + E)$. Parallel package lowering scales as $``O((V + E) / N_{\text{max\_workers}} + \log N_{\text{max\_workers}})``$. While concurrent reachability across orthogonal regions is PSPACE/NP-complete (NP-5 in REQ-0124), structured DAG workflows ensure polynomial tractability. ---
                      State lowering, hierarchy construction, and structural validation execute in Complexity Class $\mathbf{P}$. For an input model with $V$ state nodes ($``V = |\mathcal{S}_{\text{all}}|``$) and $E$ transition edges: 1. Arena allocation, symbol interning, and ancestor/LCA lookups evaluate in $O(1)$ amortized time ($h \le 32$). 2. Containment tree acyclicity and region partitioning validate in $O(V)$ time via Tarjan SCC algorithm. 3. Cross-region transition legality checks verify region membership in $O(1)$ time per transition, totaling $O(E)$ time. Total sequential verification complexity is $O(V + E)$, grounding the tractable polynomial fragment of NP-5. Parallel package lowering scales as $``O((V + E) / N_{\text{max\_workers}} + \log N_{\text{max\_workers}})``$. ---
